<p>Albertville sits on Sand Mountain, where the clay soil doesn't absorb water well and the terrain can make drainage tricky. When gutters fail — whether from clogs, leaks, or just old age — water ends up where it shouldn't: pooling around foundations, eroding landscaping, and soaking into siding and fascia boards.</p>

<p>Many Albertville homes were built in the 60s, 70s, and 80s, and still have original gutters that are well past their useful life. Others have gutters that were installed as cheaply as possible — undersized, poorly sloped, or made from materials that don't hold up to Alabama weather.</p>

<p>We see it all the time: homeowners who've been putting up with overflowing gutters for years, not realizing that a relatively affordable fix now could prevent thousands in foundation or water damage down the road. That's not a sales pitch — it's just how gutters work.</p>

